🔹 🎯 Task 1: Class with Multiple Constructors

You are building a Person class that can be instantiated in multiple ways: with full details (name and age), with just a name (age defaults), or with no arguments (both default).

📥 Input:
String name;
int age;

⚙️ Requirements:
• Create a Person class with three constructor overloads:
  - Person(String name, int age) - explicit values
  - Person(String name) - age defaults to 18
  - Person() - name defaults to "Unknown", age defaults to 0
• Add a display() method that prints: "Name: <name>, Age: <age>"
• In main(), create three Person objects using each constructor
• Call display() on each object

⚠️ Watch out:
• In JS, you'd use default parameters in the function signature. Java has no default parameters — use constructor overloading instead.
• Remember: constructor names MUST match the class name exactly, and Java is case-sensitive.

📤 Output format:
Name: <name1>, Age: <age1>
Name: <name2>, Age: <age2>
Name: <name3>, Age: <age3>

📌 Test cases:
Input: Person p1 = new Person("Alice", 25); Person p2 = new Person("Bob"); Person p3 = new Person();
Output:
Name: Alice, Age: 25
Name: Bob, Age: 18
Name: Unknown, Age: 0

Input: Person p1 = new Person("Charlie", 30); Person p2 = new Person("Diana"); Person p3 = new Person();
Output:
Name: Charlie, Age: 30
Name: Diana, Age: 18
Name: Unknown, Age: 0

Input: Person p1 = new Person("Eve", 5); Person p2 = new Person("Frank"); Person p3 = new Person();
Output:
Name: Eve, Age: 5
Name: Frank, Age: 18
Name: Unknown, Age: 0

Input: Person p1 = new Person("Grace", 100); Person p2 = new Person("Henry"); Person p3 = new Person();
Output:
Name: Grace, Age: 100
Name: Henry, Age: 18
Name: Unknown, Age: 0

