🔹 🎯 Task 2: Constructor Chaining with this()

You are refactoring the Person class from Task 1 to avoid code duplication by using constructor chaining with this().

📥 Input:
String name;
int age;

⚙️ Requirements:
• Create a Person class with three constructors:
  - Person(String name, int age) - the "full" constructor with explicit logic
  - Person(String name) - calls this(name, 18) to delegate to the full constructor
  - Person() - calls this("Unknown") to delegate to the one-arg constructor
• Add a getInfo() method that returns a String: "Person{name='<name>', age=<age>}"
• In main(), create three Person objects using each constructor and print their getInfo()

⚠️ Watch out:
• The this() call MUST be the first statement in the constructor — no code before it.
• In JS, you'd use super() in class inheritance or call a shared helper. In Java, this() is specifically for constructor delegation within the same class.
• Do not duplicate initialization code — that defeats the purpose of chaining.

📤 Output format:
Person{name='<name1>', age=<age1>}
Person{name='<name2>', age=<age2>}
Person{name='<name3>', age=<age3>}

📌 Test cases:
Input: Person p1 = new Person("Alice", 25); Person p2 = new Person("Bob"); Person p3 = new Person();
Output:
Person{name='Alice', age=25}
Person{name='Bob', age=18}
Person{name='Unknown', age=0}

Input: Person p1 = new Person("Charlie", 42); Person p2 = new Person("Diana"); Person p3 = new Person();
Output:
Person{name='Charlie', age=42}
Person{name='Diana', age=18}
Person{name='Unknown', age=0}

Input: Person p1 = new Person("Eve", 1); Person p2 = new Person("Frank"); Person p3 = new Person();
Output:
Person{name='Eve', age=1}
Person{name='Frank', age=18}
Person{name='Unknown', age=0}

Input: Person p1 = new Person("Grace", 99); Person p2 = new Person("Henry"); Person p3 = new Person();
Output:
Person{name='Grace', age=99}
Person{name='Henry', age=18}
Person{name='Unknown', age=0}

