🔥 🎯 Bonus Task: Complex Constructor with Multiple Validation Rules

You are building a User class for an authentication system with strict validation in the constructor.

📥 Input:
String username;
String email;
int age;

⚙️ Requirements:
• Create a User class with:
  - Private fields: username, email, age, userId (auto-generated)
  - Constructor User(String username, String email, int age) that validates:
    - username is not empty and is alphanumeric (no spaces or special chars) — if invalid, print "Invalid username" and set to "guest"
    - email contains "@" and ends with ".com" or ".org" — if invalid, print "Invalid email" and set to "noemail@test.com"
    - age is between 13 and 120 — if invalid, print "Invalid age" and set to 18
    - userId is auto-generated as "USER_" + username.toUpperCase() + "_" + (age * 1000)
  - Method status() that prints: "User: <username> | Email: <email> | Age: <age> | ID: <userId>"
• In main(), create five User objects with various valid and invalid combinations

⚠️ Watch out:
• String validation in Java requires methods like .contains(), .endsWith(), .matches(regex) — no simple string interpolation like JS.
• Multiple validation points in one constructor can get messy. Plan your error messages carefully.
• Age ranges require two comparison operators: age >= 13 && age <= 120 — easy to flip the logic.
• String immutability: .toUpperCase() returns a new String; the original is unchanged.

📤 Output format:
Invalid username
Invalid email
Invalid age
User: <username> | Email: <email> | Age: <age> | ID: <userId>
(repeated for each object)

